from core import L
L(4,'bfs-dfs','BFS, DFS, cycles and topological ordering','BFS;DFS;Graph traversals;Topological order','Queues, stacks and adjacency lists.',
'A traversal marks each vertex once but chooses a different order for the frontier. BFS explores by distance in edges; DFS explores one path and then backtracks. That order determines what additional information you can safely infer.',
'''BFS levels::Start source distance at zero, mark it visited and enqueue it. Remove a vertex, then discover its unvisited outgoing neighbours with distance one greater. Mark when enqueuing, not only on dequeue, to prevent repeated queued copies. Inductively, all distance-k vertices are processed before distance-(k+1) vertices, so first discovery gives minimum edge count in an unweighted graph.
||DFS structure::Record a vertex as active on entry and finished on exit. A directed edge to an active ancestor is a back edge and proves a cycle. In an undirected graph, the edge back to the DFS parent is not by itself a cycle. DFS trees depend on start vertex and neighbour order, but reachability does not.
||Topological order::A directed acyclic graph has an ordering putting every edge source before its destination. Reverse DFS finishing order gives one after confirming acyclicity. Alternatively Kahn's algorithm repeatedly removes zero-indegree vertices. If fewer than V vertices are removed, a cycle remains. Multiple valid topological orders may exist.''',
'''BFS on edges A–B,A–C,B–D,C–D from A::Enqueue A at distance 0; discover B,C at distance 1.~Processing B discovers D at distance 2; mark it immediately.~Processing C does not reenqueue D. A shortest A-to-D route uses 2 edges.
||Topological order for A→C,B→C,C→D::A and B initially have indegree zero.~Remove them in either order; only then does C become available.~Remove C, then D. A,B,C,D and B,A,C,D are both valid.''',
'BFS edge-count shortest paths do not solve arbitrary weighted paths.||In an undirected DFS, skip the parent edge in cycle detection.',
'Queue→BFS; stack/recursion→DFS.||Reverse finish order topologically sorts a DAG.',
[('In the BFS example, what is the distance in edges from A to D?',2,'D is discovered from a distance-1 neighbour.','A→B→D or A→C→D uses two edges.'),('For edges A→C,B→C,C→D, how many valid topological orders exist?',2,'Only the relative order of A and B is free.','The orders are A,B,C,D and B,A,C,D, so 2.')])
L(4,'minimum-spanning','Minimum spanning trees: cuts, Kruskal and Prim','Minimum spanning trees;Kruskal;Prim;Cut property;Disjoint sets','Weighted undirected graphs and greedy proofs.',
'An MST connects every vertex with minimum total edge weight and no cycles. It is a global network-design objective. The cut property gives a safe local choice and makes greedy algorithms possible.',
'''Cut and cycle properties::A cut partitions vertices into two nonempty sets; a crossing edge joins the sides. A lightest crossing edge is safe for some MST compatible with current choices under the standard cut argument. If it is uniquely lightest, it belongs to every MST. A uniquely heaviest edge on a cycle cannot belong to any MST. Equal weights require care: several trees may have equal optimal total weight.
||Kruskal::Sort all edges increasingly. Maintain a disjoint-set structure for chosen components. Add an edge if its endpoints are in different components and union them; otherwise reject the cycle-closing edge. Stop at V−1 chosen edges for connected input. Sorting usually dominates at O(E log E).
||Prim::Start at any vertex and repeatedly pick a cheapest edge connecting the current tree to an outside vertex. Maintain best connection keys using a priority queue. This resembles Dijkstra operationally but keys represent cheapest incident attachment, not full source distance. With a binary heap and adjacency lists, a common bound is O(E log V).''',
'''Kruskal on AB=1,BC=2,AC=3,CD=4,BD=5::Take AB then BC, connecting A,B,C.~Reject AC because it closes a cycle within that component.~Take CD to attach D. Three selected edges total 1+2+4=7.
||Use the cycle property in a triangle weighted 2,4,9::The 9 edge is uniquely heaviest on the cycle.~Removing it still leaves the triangle vertices connectable.~An MST uses edges 2 and 4 for total 6, so 9 cannot be in an MST.''',
'Prim keys are not Dijkstra source distances.||Equal edge weights can yield multiple MSTs.',
'Kruskal grows components; Prim grows one tree.||MST has V−1 edges on connected input.',
[('What is the MST total weight in the four-vertex Kruskal example?',7,'Add the accepted edges only.','1+2+4=7; AC and BD are rejected/unneeded.'),('What is MST weight for a triangle with edge weights 2,4,9?',6,'Choose the two lighter edges.','2+4=6 connects all three vertices without a cycle.')])
L(4,'shortest-paths','Dijkstra, Bellman–Ford and Floyd–Warshall','Shortest paths;Dijkstra;Bellman Ford;Floyd Warshall','Graph relaxation, heaps and dynamic programming.',
'Shortest-path algorithms differ mainly in how they schedule relaxation and which edge weights their proofs tolerate. Treat unreachable distances as infinity and preserve path predecessors if you need a route rather than just its cost.',
'''Dijkstra finalization::Initialize source to 0 and others to infinity. Extract the unfinalized minimum tentative distance, then relax its outgoing edges. Nonnegative edges ensure no later route through a farther vertex can improve the finalized vertex. A reachable negative edge breaks that proof. Zero weights are allowed. Binary-heap adjacency-list implementations commonly take O((V+E)log V).
||Bellman–Ford::Relax all edges up to V−1 rounds. After round k, shortest paths using at most k edges have been accounted for in the standard formulation. A simple shortest path uses at most V−1 edges when no reachable negative cycle improves it. Another improving pass identifies a reachable negative cycle; not every negative edge is a negative cycle.
||Floyd–Warshall::Let Dᵏ[i,j] allow internal vertices only from the first k. Transition is min(Dᵏ⁻¹[i,j],Dᵏ⁻¹[i,k]+Dᵏ⁻¹[k,j]). It either avoids k or routes through k. Use k as the outer loop in the ordinary in-place implementation, with O(V³) time and O(V²) storage. Negative diagonal values after completion detect relevant negative cycles.''',
'''Dijkstra from A with AB=2,AC=7,BC=1::After processing A, tentative B=2,C=7.~Finalize B next and relax BC to obtain C=3.~Finalize C at 3; predecessor route is A,B,C.
||A negative edge defeating naive Dijkstra::Edges A→B=1,A→C=2,C→B=−3.~Finalizing B at 1 before C would appear safe under the nonnegative proof.~But A→C→B costs −1. Bellman–Ford can discover that improvement; usual Dijkstra assumptions fail.''',
'A negative edge is not itself a negative cycle.||Only reachable negative cycles affect a single-source problem.',
'Dijkstra needs nonnegative weights.||Bellman–Ford checks an extra relaxation round.||Floyd–Warshall outer loop is intermediate vertex.',
[('In the Dijkstra example AB=2,AC=7,BC=1, what is shortest distance A to C?',3,'Compare direct with via B.','min(7,2+1)=3.'),('For A→C=2 and C→B=−3, what is that route’s total cost?',-1,'Negative weights still add arithmetically.','2−3=−1.')])
L(7,'files','File systems, allocation and free-space management','File systems;Contiguous allocation;Linked allocation;Indexed allocation;Inodes','Disk blocks and pointers.',
'A file system connects human names and byte offsets to physical blocks. File metadata, block allocation and directory structure have different responsibilities. A lookup cost depends on which metadata is cached and which indirection levels must be read.',
'''Allocation choices::Contiguous allocation stores consecutive blocks and computes block location by start+index, but growth and external fragmentation are challenges. Linked allocation stores the next block address in each block or a separate table; random access requires following links unless indexed metadata helps. Indexed allocation keeps a pointer array or tree so a chosen logical block can be located without reading all previous data blocks.
||Direct and indirect pointers::An inode-style record can have several direct pointers, a pointer to one indirect block of addresses, and further levels. If a block has B bytes and an address occupies a bytes, an indirect block holds B/a pointers ignoring metadata. A double-indirect path can cover (B/a)² data blocks. Access cost includes metadata blocks that are not already cached.
||Directories and consistency::Directory entries connect names to file identifiers; hard links can give multiple names for the same file object while symbolic links contain a path to be resolved. Free-space bitmaps or lists track available blocks. Updates spanning allocation, data and metadata need crash consistency, often via journaling or copy-on-write techniques. File permissions control access independently of block placement.''',
'''Capacity with 10 direct pointers plus one indirect pointer::Use 1024-byte blocks and 4-byte addresses, so indirect capacity=1024/4=256 addresses.~Total reachable data blocks=10+256=266.~Maximum data capacity under this simplified structure=266×1024=272384 bytes, excluding metadata itself.
||Random access under linked allocation::To reach logical block 4, start at logical block 0.~Follow four next-block links unless a separate cached index supplies the location.~Contiguous allocation computes its address directly; the representation changes cost.''',
'An indirect block is metadata, not an additional file-data block.||Count cached and uncached metadata according to the question.',
'Pointers per index block=block bytes/pointer bytes.||Directories map names; allocation maps logical data to blocks.',
[('A 4096-byte index block uses 8-byte pointers without overhead. How many pointers fit?',512,'Divide bytes by pointer size.','4096/8=512 pointers.'),('A file uses 3 data blocks of 1024 bytes. What is allocated data capacity?',3072,'Multiply block count by size.','3×1024=3072 bytes; actual file length may be smaller.')])
L(9,'sockets','The socket API and TCP byte-stream framing','Socket API;bind;listen;accept;connect;send;recv','TCP, processes and blocking I/O.',
'A socket is a programming interface to network communication. Establishing a connection and exchanging application messages are separate tasks. TCP delivers a byte stream, so the application must decide how to recognize message boundaries.',
'''Server lifecycle::Create a socket, bind it to a local address/port, listen for incoming connections and accept. accept returns a connected socket for one peer while the listening socket remains for future connections. A server can serve multiple clients using threads, processes or nonblocking/event-driven I/O. Privileged-port and address-binding rules depend on the OS.
||Client and data transfer::A TCP client creates a socket and calls connect to the server endpoint. send/recv may transfer fewer bytes than requested; robust code loops as needed. A recv returning zero for a TCP stream indicates an orderly peer closure after available data is exhausted. Errors and timeouts need handling rather than assuming every operation completes immediately.
||Application framing::One send call need not equal one recv call. Define a length prefix, delimiter with escaping, fixed record size or another protocol framing scheme. Accumulate incoming bytes until one complete message is available, retain surplus bytes for the next message, and bound lengths to avoid unbounded allocation. Network byte order converts multibyte integer fields consistently.''',
'''Send a length-prefixed message “hello”::Encode length 5 in the agreed prefix format.~Transmit the prefix and five payload bytes, allowing for partial writes.~The receiver first accumulates the complete prefix, then exactly five payload bytes, even if several recv calls are needed.
||A recv returns 3 bytes while 8 remain in the protocol message::Append the 3 bytes to the message buffer.~The remaining expected payload decreases to 5.~Call recv again as necessary; the short read alone is not a protocol failure.''',
'accept returns a new connected socket.||send/recv boundaries are not message boundaries.',
'TCP is a byte stream.||Explicit framing and partial-I/O handling belong to the application.',
[('A framed payload needs 12 bytes and 7 have arrived. How many remain?',5,'Track expected length minus received length.','12−7=5 bytes remain.'),('One listening socket accepts 3 connections that remain open. How many socket objects are present, including the listener?',4,'Count the listener plus each accepted socket.','1+3=4 sockets.')])
L(9,'dns','DNS hierarchy, resolution and caching','DNS;Recursive resolution;Iterative resolution;TTL;Resource records','IP addresses and application/transport layers.',
'DNS distributes naming information across a hierarchy rather than storing every name in one central server. Resolution may require several queries, but caching avoids repeating most of that work until records expire.',
'''Names and authority::A domain name is a sequence of labels. Root servers direct queries toward relevant top-level-domain servers, which direct them toward authoritative servers for delegated zones. An authoritative answer describes data in that zone; a recursive resolver acts for a client and obtains/caches answers. Delegation boundaries and host labels are not interchangeable concepts.
||Query behaviour::In recursive resolution, the requester asks a resolver to return a final answer or failure. The resolver can use iterative queries, following referrals rather than asking each upstream server to do the whole lookup. Cached records have TTL lifetimes. Caching both answers and referrals can reduce later queries; a cached IP may expire before or after related delegation records.
||Records and transport::A records map names to IPv4 addresses; AAAA to IPv6; CNAME expresses aliases; MX identifies mail exchangers; NS identifies name servers. DNS commonly uses UDP port 53 for ordinary queries and can use TCP, including for larger/truncated responses and zone transfers under their relevant rules. DNS is not always UDP, and a domain can map to more than one address.''',
'''Resolve an uncached name conceptually::A client asks its recursive resolver.~The resolver follows root and TLD referrals to a relevant authoritative server.~It returns the answer and caches permitted records for their TTLs; future queries can skip steps if caches are valid.
||A cached record has TTL 120 seconds and was stored 45 seconds ago::Assuming no refresh or eviction, remaining valid lifetime is 75 seconds.~A query before expiry can use the cached record.~After expiry, the resolver must obtain fresh information rather than trusting that TTL indefinitely.''',
'Recursive resolver and authoritative server have different roles.||DNS also uses TCP.',
'A→IPv4; AAAA→IPv6; CNAME→alias; MX→mail.||TTL controls cache freshness.',
[('TTL was 180 seconds and 70 seconds have elapsed. What is remaining lifetime?',110,'Subtract elapsed time.','180−70=110 seconds.'),('How many bits are in the address returned by an IPv4 A record?',32,'Use IPv4 address width.','IPv4 addresses contain 32 bits.')])
L(9,'http','HTTP requests, responses and persistent connections','HTTP;Methods;Status codes;Persistence;Caching','Sockets, DNS and transport.',
'HTTP defines application-level meaning: which resource is requested, what action is asked for and how the response is described. Its semantics are separate from the transport connection and remain recognizable across protocol versions.',
'''Request and response structure::A request carries a method, target and headers, often with a body. GET retrieves a representation; POST commonly submits processing data; PUT replaces at a known target under its semantics. A response includes a status code, headers and possibly a body. Status classes 2xx indicate success, 3xx redirection, 4xx client-side request problems and 5xx server-side failures.
||Persistence and latency::Persistent connections reuse transport for multiple requests, reducing repeated setup. HTTP/1.1 generally supports persistent connections; HTTP/2 multiplexes streams within its transport model. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP rather than TCP. A latency question must specify DNS/cache state, connection establishment, transfer size, request dependencies and whether connections can be reused.
||Caching and security::Cache-Control and related metadata control reuse; conditional requests can validate existing content rather than redownload it. HTTPS protects HTTP exchange using the applicable secure transport, but does not change what GET or a status code means. An HTTP cache and a DNS cache store different information and can have unrelated expiry times.''',
'''Interpret an HTTP 404 response::The server produced a valid protocol response.~Its status is in the 4xx category and indicates the requested resource was not found under the response semantics.~This is not the same as a TCP connection refusal, where no HTTP exchange may have occurred.
||Compare three requests with/without transport reuse::If each new TCP connection costs one setup RTT, three separate connections pay three setup RTTs in this simplified model.~One persistent connection pays one setup RTT.~Two setup RTTs are saved; actual response delays and transfers must still be included.''',
'Connection refusal is not an HTTP error response.||HTTP/3 does not use TCP.',
'2xx success; 3xx redirect; 4xx request issue; 5xx server failure.||Persistence reduces repeated setup.',
[('What is the hundred-class digit of HTTP status 503?',5,'Divide into the status family.','503 belongs to 5xx, whose class digit is 5.'),('Three separate one-RTT setups are replaced by one reused connection. How many setup RTTs are saved?',2,'Compare three with one.','3−1=2 setup RTTs saved under the stated model.')])
